}

\section{Output}{

The returned bundle has class \code{mfrm_manifest} and includes:
\itemize{
\item \code{summary}: one-row analysis overview
\item \code{readiness}, \code{readiness_components}, and \code{readiness_parameters}: the
fitted model's recorded numerical checks and restrictions on inference,
overall and by component or parameter
\item \code{environment}: package/R/platform metadata
\item \code{model_settings}: key-value model settings table
\item \code{source_columns}: key-value data-column table
\item \code{estimation_control}: key-value optimizer settings table
\item \code{anchor_summary}: facet-level anchor summary. \code{model_settings} separately
records \code{centering_scope} and \code{noncenter_facet}; missing convention metadata
in a native fit retains the legacy \code{"remainder"} interpretation
\item \code{anchors}: machine-readable anchor table
\item \code{hierarchical_review}: retained traceability table for hierarchical /
small-sample design flags
\item \code{missing_recoding}: retained traceability table for missing-code recoding
\item \code{shrinkage_review}: retained traceability table for shrinkage settings
\item \code{available_outputs}: availability table for diagnostics/bias/PCA/prediction
outputs
\item \code{dependencies}, \code{input_summary}, and \code{session_info}: reproducibility
metadata tables. \code{input_summary} records object structure and does not
claim byte-for-byte identity across platforms or serialization formats
\item \code{settings}: manifest build settings
}
}

\section{Interpreting output}{

Read \code{summary} to identify the analysis, then \code{available_outputs} for existing
diagnostics/bias/PCA/predictions and \code{anchors} for fixed constraints.
\code{model_settings}, \code{source_columns} and \code{estimation_control} support the
methods write-up.

\code{Converged} records the optimizer return-code decision; \code{InferenceReady}
records whether the fit passes the additional numerical checks required for
inference. They need not agree; passing these checks does not establish model
fit or the statistical adequacy of every interval method.
The \code{readiness} tables preserve these checks for the original fitted model,
including the check-format version and reason codes. Re-running the analysis
must repeat the checks using the new fit; the saved records do not certify it.
Active latent-regression fits also record their population-model provenance
there, including the fitted scoring basis, stored \code{population_formula}, and
person-level data requirements used by the fitted population model. When categorical
background variables are expanded through \code{stats::model.matrix()},
\code{population_xlevel_variables} and \code{population_contrast_variables} identify
the variables whose fitted coding must be preserved for replay/scoring.


The returned object contains:
\itemize{
\item \code{summary}: a one-row overview of the chosen replay mode and whether bundle
export was included
\item \code{script}: the generated R code as a single string
\item \code{source_readiness}, \code{source_readiness_components}, and
\code{source_readiness_parameters}: readiness provenance from the source fit
\item \code{anchors} and \code{group_anchors}: the exact stored constraints that were
embedded into the script
}

The source readiness row is not copied into the replayed fit. The generated
script recomputes readiness and warns when its fit-level decision fields do
not match the source record.
Identifier columns are read as text, preserving leading zeros and literal
\code{"NA"} labels. Original column names and factor coding in exported
person-level background data are retained. Stored diagnostic settings are
replayed as well; older diagnostic objects may not retain interaction
selection or PCA limits, so rerun \code{\link[=diagnose_mfrm]{diagnose_mfrm()}} before exporting them
when those settings matter.
}

\section{Typical workflow}{

Build from a fit and reuse its diagnostics. To control residual PCA,
call \code{\link[=diagnose_mfrm]{diagnose_mfrm()}} first. \code{\link[=export_mfrm_bundle]{export_mfrm_bundle()}} writes the manifest
and selected analysis files to disk.

For \code{GPCM} fits, the manifest is available with an explicit
\code{gpcm_boundary} table. It records supported direct diagnostics/reporting
surfaces while keeping full FACETS score-side contract review blocked and
routing design forecasting through its separate caveated capability row.


\enumerate{
\item Finalize a fit and diagnostics object.
\item Generate the replay script with the path you want users to read from.
\item Write \code{replay$script} to disk, or let \code{\link[=export_mfrm_bundle]{export_mfrm_bundle()}} do it for
you.
\item Rerun the script in a fresh R session to confirm reproducibility.
}
}

\section{Saved-result reuse}{

Supplied screening results must match this analysis; older ones without
identity metadata are refused. See "Saved-result reuse" in
\code{\link[=export_mfrm_bundle]{export_mfrm_bundle()}} for calibration checks, separate scenario forecasts and
recreating compatible results without refitting.


Supplied screening results must match this analysis; older ones without
identity metadata are refused. See "Saved-result reuse" in
\code{\link[=export_mfrm_bundle]{export_mfrm_bundle()}} for calibration checks, separate scenario forecasts and
recreating compatible results without refitting.


Supplied screening results must describe this fitted analysis. Results from
another fit or older results without identity metadata are refused. See
\link{mfrmr_compatibility_layer} to recreate them from the saved fit and matching
diagnostics without refitting the model.
Supplied unit scores, plausible values and fit-derived forecasts must also
match this calibration. A forecast created with \code{sim_spec} remains a separate
scenario; its source is recorded without asserting a match to the fit.
}

\section{Mode guide}{

\itemize{
\item \code{"auto"} is the safest default and follows the structure of the supplied
object.
\item \code{"fit"} is useful when you want a minimal script centered on
\code{\link[=fit_mfrm]{fit_mfrm()}}.
\item \code{"facets"} is useful when you want to preserve the higher-level
\code{\link[=run_mfrm_facets]{run_mfrm_facets()}} workflow, including stored column mapping.
}
}

\section{Choose a save or export route}{

\itemize{
\item \strong{Reopen without refitting:} \code{\link[base:saveRDS]{base::saveRDS()}} / \code{\link[base:readRDS]{base::readRDS()}} preserves
an R object. For an existing \code{\link[=mfrm_results]{mfrm_results()}} bundle,
\code{\link[=export_mfrm_results]{export_mfrm_results()}} includes the RDS and a reload script by default.
\item \strong{Write tables or an analysis folder:} \code{\link[=export_mfrm_tables]{export_mfrm_tables()}} writes core
CSVs without automatic diagnostics. \code{export_mfrm_bundle()} writes the
selected artifacts below; \code{\link[=build_mfrm_manifest]{build_mfrm_manifest()}} records settings and
available outputs.
\item \strong{Rerun the analysis:} \code{\link[=build_mfrm_replay_script]{build_mfrm_replay_script()}} generates R code for
a new fit. The \code{"script"} component here writes that recipe, not the fitted
R object. Generating the script does not execute it.
}
}

\section{Recommended presets}{

Common starting points are:
\itemize{
\item minimal tables: \code{include = c("core_tables", "manifest")}
\item reporting bundle: \code{include = c("core_tables", "checklist", "dashboard", "summary_tables", "html")}
\item archival bundle: \code{include = c("core_tables", "manifest", "script", "visual_summaries", "html")}
}
}

\section{Written outputs}{

\code{include} selects these components:
\itemize{
\item \code{"core_tables"}: CSVs from \code{\link[=export_mfrm_tables]{export_mfrm_tables()}}.
\item \code{"checklist"}: CSVs from \link{mfrm_report_checklist}.
\item \code{"dashboard"}: \code{\link[=facet_quality_dashboard]{facet_quality_dashboard()}} tables and interpretation notes.
CSV/HTML retain screening settings; HTML shows every level's estimates and
unavailable diagnostics, while CSV retains full detail.
\item \code{"apa"}: text from \code{\link[=build_apa_outputs]{build_apa_outputs()}}.
\item \code{"summary_tables"}: manuscript CSVs and indexes from \code{\link[=build_summary_table_bundle]{build_summary_table_bundle()}}.
\item \code{"anchors"}: CSV from \code{\link[=make_anchor_table]{make_anchor_table()}}.
\item \code{"manifest"}: CSV/TXT from \code{\link[=build_mfrm_manifest]{build_mfrm_manifest()}}.
\item \code{"visual_summaries"}: warnings/residual PCA summaries from \code{\link[=build_visual_summaries]{build_visual_summaries()}}.
\item \code{"predictions"}: CSVs for supplied \code{\link[=predict_mfrm_population]{predict_mfrm_population()}},
\code{\link[=predict_mfrm_units]{predict_mfrm_units()}} or \code{\link[=sample_mfrm_plausible_values]{sample_mfrm_plausible_values()}} results.
\item \code{"script"}: code from \code{\link[=build_mfrm_replay_script]{build_mfrm_replay_script()}} and, when available,
the latent-regression replay person-data CSV.
\item \code{"html"}: exported tables/text and a replay-sidecar artifact index;
raw person-level replay rows are not embedded. This is not deidentification.
}

Readiness CSVs preserve source-fit checks, latent-regression exports retain
replay \code{sim_spec} metadata when predictions are supplied, and \code{GPCM} bundles
record \code{gpcm_boundary}. Supplied diagnostics, screening tables, unit scores,
and plausible values must match \code{fit}'s calibration identity; inspect
\code{bundle$summary} and \code{bundle$written_files}.
}

